  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Near-severe scattered thunderstorms over the NW \nwill be on a gradual down/weaking trend for the rest of the \nnight. \n\nStorms continue to roll in from western PA at 10 PM EDT. Some \nwere more-potent over the last 3 hours, but have been on the \ndown-trend as we have lost the heating of the day. Synoptic\nforcing is generating additional storms at this time, and a\nflash flood threat has begun for the places that recived heavier\nrain earlier iun the evening. Rain gauge in Grand Valley has\nover 4\" this evening. If that is accurate, the radar estimates\nare underdoing what has fallen. Too sparse of coverage to go \nwith a flood watch at this point. Will cover what needs to be \ncovered with timely warnings.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Mainly dry for Sunday, though daily chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms return to the forecast for next week.\n\nUpper level flow becomes more zonal for Sunday which should \nresult in a mainly dry day, outside of an isolated shower or \nstorm that develops on the higher terrain of western PA. The \nbreak from the rain will be short-lived, however, as multiple \nshortwaves moving through will bring daily chances for showers \nand storms as we head into the coming week. \n\nMachine learning ensembles suggest that there will be some \nsevere weather potential for at least a couple of days next week\nand SPC has much of the region outlooked in a marginal risk for\nMonday. There will also be some potential for flash flooding as\nwell, and WPC has placed all of central PA in a marginal risk \nfor both Monday and Tuesday. There is still some uncertainty \nregarding the severe and flash flood risks, but the details will\nbe worked out over the coming days.",
